  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: S&EDisc 6
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: 'At a weekly lab meeting, your research team discusses early results
    from several drug compounds being tested in cancer cell samples. A new student
    researcher, who recently joined the lab from another institution, notices that
    one of the compounds is showing promising early results. He wonders whether these
    early findings need to be reviewed by a scientific committee now, even though
    the work is limited to only discovery-stage lab experiments and tests.


    What
    does a Scientific Review Committee (SRC) do?'
  explanation: A Scientific Review Committee (SRC) focuses on making sure the research
    plan is scientifically solid before it moves into more advanced stages of clinical
    research, especially before human trials begin. They do not usually review early
    discovery work, like basic testing in dishes of cancer cells in the lab. However,
    even in the discovery stage, the SRC helps ensure that research is conducted
    safely and ethically, following accepted standards.
  correctAnswer: "A: They thoroughly review the research plan before human trials,
    but they usually don\u2019t review early discovery work. "
  incorrectAnswer1: 'B: They approve or deny every research idea before any testing
    begins at any stage of clinical research.'
  incorrectAnswer2: 'C: They only evaluate the research after the FDA has given full
    approval for a new cancer drug.'
  incorrectAnswer3: 'D: None of the above.'
  questionRole: Safety & Ethics
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: Discovery
